Leah, I normally like your columns but you are way off on this one which leads me to ask if you have any training in scientific evaluation..  Here are some rebuttals for you:
* aromatherapy has been shown via MRIs to induce relaxation;
* essential oils used in aromatherapy are capable of killing cancer (NIH);
* Reiki is a powerful coping strategy to help trauma victims process trauma.  The second level of Reiki training involves eye movements which actually release tension from the eyes and can center a person. There is science around this.
*acupuncture cannot be validated via  statistical methodologies because of flaws in the methodologies;
*chemotherapy may be scientifically proven but it is based on a war model whereby not only the bad cells get killed but so do the good ones (the side effects can be horrific); as a result the medical profession is looking more to make the host healthy (immunotherapy).
Good science is a tool in the tool kit. If woo woo reduces stress it can help.

That does not have the political advantage of the knee-jerk reaction laws, just so the gun grabbers can claim that they did something.. It was even in the article;
"In Colorado, we were pleased to support the successful ban in 2013 of ammunition magazines that held more than 15 rounds."
Without citing one single shred of evidence that it even saved one life in 4 years. Pathetic.

